    "textContent":  "#!/bin/bash\n\n# Set the desired image name and size\nIMAGE_NAME=\"MyNet_SysPrint.img\"\nIMAGE_SIZE=\"1TB\"\n\n# Print a message to the console\necho \"Creating blank image...\"\n\n# Create the blank image file\ndd if=/dev/zero of=\"$IMAGE_NAME\" bs=1M count=$(echo \"$IMAGE_SIZE\" | sed \u0027s/[^0-9]*//\u0027)\n\n# Print a success message\necho \"Image created successfully!\"\n\n# 2. Partition the image (using parted)\necho \"Partitioning the image...\"\nsudo parted -s \"$IMAGE_NAME\" mklabel msdos\nsudo parted -s \"$IMAGE_NAME\" mkpart primary \"$FILE_SYSTEM\" 1MiB 100%\n\n# 3. Format the partition with the specified file system\necho \"Formatting the partition...\"\nsudo kpartx -a -v \"$IMAGE_NAME\"  # Map the partitions\nPARTITION_DEVICE=$(sudo kpartx -l \"$IMAGE_NAME\" | awk \u0027{print \"/dev/mapper/\"$1\"p1\"}\u0027) # Get the partition device\nsudo mkfs.\"$FILE_SYSTEM\" \"$PARTITION_DEVICE\"\n\n# 4. Mount the partition to a mount point\necho \"Mounting the partition...\"\nMOUNT_POINT=\"./my_image_mount\"\nmkdir -p \"$MOUNT_POINT\"\nsudo mount \"$PARTITION_DEVICE\" \"$MOUNT_POINT\"\n\n# 5. Use the mounted file system (example: create a file)\necho \"Creating a test file...\"\nsudo touch \"$MOUNT_POINT/testfile.txt\"\n\n# 6. Unmount and cleanup\necho \"Unmounting and cleaning up...\"\nsudo umount \"$MOUNT_POINT\"\nsudo kpartx -d \"$IMAGE_NAME\"\necho \"Done! The file system is created in $IMAGE_NAME and mounted to $MOUNT_POINT.\"\n\n# Optional: To access the image later, you can mount it again:\n# sudo kpartx -a -v my_image.img\n# sudo mount /dev/mapper/loop0p1 ./my_image_mount\n# ... work with the files ...\n# sudo umount ./my_image_mount\n# sudo kpartx -d my_image.img",
    "title":  "silesys-image",
